% holding-blocked: Source check: the song's name and language, whether it is a customary (kastom) song of a clan or a newer song with a known maker, and whether the group would let it be taught; ask the Dampet Culture Group through Helen Taivii Sapien. Transcription: the parts could not be read from this outdoor recording (many voices, ukulele, wind and crowd, pitch drifting); a cleaner recording, or the group's help, is needed for the harmony.
% holding-kind: fragment
% holding-kind-note: A record of the clip and its pulse only; missing: the melody and the harmony parts, the words, the song's name and owners, the ukulele's chords, and the dance.
% source-url: https://www.instagram.com/p/Der8fSRGSJC/?img_index=20 Dust-to-Digital on Instagram (posted 19 October 2026), clip 20 of 20: the Dampet Culture Group performing in Kavieng, New Ireland, Papua New Guinea, via Helen Taivii Sapien
% performance-url: https://www.instagram.com/p/Der8fSRGSJC/?img_index=20 the Dampet Culture Group, Kavieng, New Ireland (Dust-to-Digital, October 2026, via Helen Taivii Sapien; the video is dated 10.07.26)
% performance-note: The clip is 60 seconds, outdoors on gravel. Helen Taivii Sapien's Facebook post is the original. For New Ireland singing: the Institute of Papua New Guinea Studies (Port Moresby) holds field recordings, and the group may have its own videos.
% dossier-function: A culture group's performance: men and women in leaf and grass costume with feathered headdresses, singing in parts and dancing in a group, one of them playing a red ukulele, for an audience (perhaps at a festival or an arrival ceremony; the clip does not say).
% dossier-elements: Many voices in close harmony around a high melody, with the ukulele strummed underneath; a steady beat with a stress on every second beat. The voices drift in pitch through the clip (a quarter-tone flat at the start, near A=440 later), as outdoor group singing does.
% dossier-practice: About 97 beats a minute (beats 0.62 s apart), the stress every 1.25 s; full voices, outdoors, sung while dancing.
% dossier-accompaniment: Tradition: voices, and in the newer style the ukulele or guitar of the string bands; older New Ireland dance songs use the kundu (hourglass drum) and the garamut (slit drum). In class: the band's chords and a soft beat, once the parts are known.
% dossier-lyrics: Not taken: not clear in the recording; the language may be Tok Pisin or one of New Ireland's own (Tigak, Kara, Nalik and others around Kavieng); to be checked by a speaker.
% dossier-honesty: Nothing of the music is transcribed here: only the pulse, the instruments and what the video shows.
% dossier-licence: Unknown: customary songs belong to clans and communities in New Ireland, and string-band songs to their makers. The recording belongs to the group and Helen Taivii Sapien; no audio or video is kept here.
% dossier-respect: Customary songs and dances in New Ireland can be tied to clans, to the malagan funerary cycle or to ceremonies; ask the group what this one is and whether it may be sung elsewhere before teaching any of it. Name the group and Kavieng; present the costumes as their own, not as a show.
% dossier-review: The Dampet Culture Group, through Helen Taivii Sapien; a New Ireland speaker of the song's language.
